# D119 Batch 3: MSK Pediatric (10) + Neonatal (15) + Mental Health Pediatric (15) + Dermatology Pediatric (15) = 55 questions
questions = [
  # ===== MSK PEDIATRIC (10) =====
  {"topic":"MSK Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 3-year-old girl cries suddenly after her father swung her by the arms. She holds her right arm pronated and slightly flexed at the elbow, refusing to use it. X-ray is normal. What is the diagnosis and treatment?","a":["Supracondylar fracture — cast","Nursemaid's elbow (radial head subluxation) — hyperpronation or supination/flexion reduction maneuver","Shoulder dislocation — conscious sedation and reduction","Brachial plexus injury — MRI"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Nursemaid's elbow (pulled elbow): radial head subluxation from longitudinal traction on arm (lifting, swinging). Classic: child crying, arm held pronated and slightly flexed, refuses to use arm, X-ray normal. Treatment: closed reduction — either hyperpronation technique (more successful: pronate the forearm fully while applying pressure over radial head) or supination/flexion. **Board tip:** Hyperpronation method has higher first-attempt success rate than supination-flexion. Successful reduction: click felt, child resumes using arm within minutes. No immobilization needed after successful reduction."},
  {"topic":"MSK Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 6-year-old obese boy presents with a painless limp and decreased internal rotation of the right hip. Hip X-ray shows flattening and fragmentation of the femoral head with widening of the joint space. What is the diagnosis?","a":["Transient synovitis of the hip","Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease (avascular necrosis of the femoral head)","Developmental dysplasia of the hip","Septic arthritis"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Legg-Calvé-Perthes (LCP): Avascular necrosis of the femoral epiphysis in children 4-10 years old, more common in boys. Presents with painless limp, decreased internal rotation/abduction. X-ray: increased density then fragmentation/flattening of femoral head. **Board tip:** Distinguish from transient synovitis (normal X-ray, fever low-grade, more acute onset, resolves in 1-2 weeks) and septic arthritis (high fever, toxic, ESR/CRP elevated, emergent drainage). LCP requires orthopedic referral."},
  {"topic":"MSK Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 13-year-old overweight boy complains of left hip and knee pain after PE class. On exam, he has limited internal rotation of the hip and his left leg appears externally rotated and shorter. X-ray shows medial displacement of the femoral head. What is the diagnosis?","a":["Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease","Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis (SCFE) — emergent orthopedic referral","Perthes disease","Transient synovitis"],"correct":1,"rationale":"SCFE: Most common hip disorder in adolescents 10-16 years. Risk factors: obesity, male, African American, hypothyroidism. Femoral head 'slips' posteriorly/medially through the growth plate. Classic: obese teen with hip/knee pain and external rotation. 'Klein line' sign on X-ray — line along superior femoral neck doesn't intersect femoral head. **Board tip:** SCFE = EMERGENCY. Non-weight-bearing and urgent orthopedic referral — bilateral in 25-40% of cases. If bilateral, screen for endocrine causes (hypothyroidism, hypogonadism, growth hormone deficiency)."},
  {"topic":"MSK Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"An 11-year-old active girl has anterior knee pain that worsens with running, kneeling, and stair climbing. She has point tenderness and a firm bump over the tibial tubercle. X-ray shows fragmentation of the tibial tubercle. What is the diagnosis?","a":["Patellar fracture","Osgood-Schlatter disease (tibial apophysitis)","Septic arthritis","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Osgood-Schlatter disease: Overuse injury of the tibial apophysis (insertion of patellar tendon on tibia), common in active adolescents during growth spurts. Classic: painful prominence over tibial tubercle, worse with activity. Treatment: activity modification, ice, NSAIDs, quadriceps stretching. Self-limiting — resolves with skeletal maturity. **Board tip:** Most common cause of knee pain in active adolescents. Bilateral in 20-30%. X-ray shows tibial tubercle fragmentation and soft tissue swelling. No surgery needed. Reassure patient — not dangerous."},
  {"topic":"MSK Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 10-year-old is referred for evaluation of spinal curvature noted at school screening. Adam's forward bend test shows a rib hump. X-ray Cobb angle is 22°. She has no back pain. What is the management?","a":["Immediate spinal surgery referral","Orthopedic referral for observation and bracing if Cobb angle is 25-40° in a growing child; monitoring with X-rays every 6 months","No treatment — all scoliosis resolves spontaneously","Physical therapy alone — spinal manipulation"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Idiopathic scoliosis management by Cobb angle: <20° = observe (X-rays every 6-12 months); 20-40° in growing child = bracing (Milwaukee or Boston brace reduces progression); >40-50° = surgical referral (spinal fusion). **Board tip:** Adam's forward bend test: positive rib hump = rotational component of scoliosis. Adolescent idiopathic scoliosis is most common (girls 4:1). Screen at 10-12 year well visit. Bracing is most effective when initiated before Risser stage 2 (early growth)."},
  {"topic":"MSK Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"Parents bring in a 3-week-old infant because his head tilts to the right and rotates to the left. On exam, a firm, non-tender mass is palpable in the right sternocleidomastoid muscle. What is the diagnosis?","a":["Clavicle fracture from birth trauma","Congenital muscular torticollis (fibromatosis colli) — physical therapy/stretching","Cervical spine fracture","Klippel-Feil syndrome — MRI"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Congenital muscular torticollis: Fibrosis of the SCM muscle from birth positioning or birth trauma. Head tilts to AFFECTED side (SCM contracts), chin rotates to OPPOSITE side. Management: physical therapy/stretching program. 90% resolve by age 1 with PT. **Board tip:** If not responding to PT by 6-12 months, consider surgical release. Always evaluate for plagiocephaly (flattening of skull) and hip dysplasia (associated conditions). Rule out cervical vertebral anomaly with X-ray if no improvement."},
  {"topic":"MSK Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 5-year-old boy has been walking on his toes exclusively for 2 years. He has no neurologic symptoms, normal strength, and full passive dorsiflexion of both ankles. What is the most likely diagnosis?","a":["Duchenne muscular dystrophy — CK level","Cerebral palsy — MRI brain","Idiopathic toe-walking — watchful waiting and stretching","Leg length discrepancy — orthopedic referral"],"correct":2,"rationale":"Idiopathic toe-walking (ITW): Diagnosis of exclusion. Present in 7-24% of toddlers/young children. Key features: bilateral, preserved passive ankle range of motion, no neurologic deficits. Most resolve spontaneously by age 3-5. **Board tip:** Red flags warranting further evaluation: unilateral toe-walking (leg length discrepancy), spasticity (cerebral palsy), Gower sign/proximal weakness (Duchenne MD), tight Achilles tendon. Always do full neurologic exam. Refer if persistent beyond age 5."},
  {"topic":"MSK Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 13-year-old male soccer player complains of heel pain that is worse with activity and at the start of the day. Point tenderness is at the posterior calcaneus (Achilles tendon insertion). X-ray shows increased density of calcaneal apophysis. What is the diagnosis?","a":["Plantar fasciitis","Sever's disease (calcaneal apophysitis)","Achilles tendon rupture","Stress fracture"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Sever's disease: Apophysitis of the calcaneal growth plate, most common cause of heel pain in children 8-14 years during growth spurts. Caused by repetitive traction of the Achilles tendon on the calcaneal apophysis. Treatment: heel cups, Achilles stretching, activity modification, ice, NSAIDs. Self-limiting — resolves with growth plate closure. **Board tip:** Similar concept to Osgood-Schlatter (tibial apophysis) but at the heel. Both are overuse apophysitis conditions in growing athletes. X-ray rules out fracture but findings alone not diagnostic."},
  {"topic":"MSK Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 4-year-old has bilateral bowing of the legs. The angle between the tibia and femur is 15 degrees bilaterally. This was also present at age 2. His diet is poor and he has minimal sun exposure. What additional finding would support a diagnosis of nutritional rickets?","a":["Leg length discrepancy","Rachitic rosary (costochondral beading), widening of wrists, elevated alkaline phosphatase, and low serum phosphorus and vitamin D","Decreased bone density on DXA scan","Elevated PTH with normal calcium"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Nutritional rickets (Vitamin D or calcium deficiency): Bowing of the legs (genu varum), rachitic rosary (enlarged costochondral junctions), craniotabes, Harrison's groove, widened wrists/ankles from physeal widening. Labs: low 25-OH vitamin D, elevated ALP and PTH, low phosphorus. **Board tip:** Physiologic genu varum resolves by age 2 and genu valgum ('knock-knees') appears 3-4 years — both normal. Persistent bowing after age 3 or severe bowing warrants evaluation for rickets or Blount disease (tibia vara)."},
  {"topic":"MSK Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 16-year-old female distance runner presents with progressive shin pain that worsens with running and is relieved by rest. Point tenderness is present over the mid-tibial shaft. X-ray is initially normal. What is the most appropriate next diagnostic step?","a":["X-ray is sufficient — diagnose as shin splints and continue training","MRI of the tibia (most sensitive) to evaluate for tibial stress fracture","EMG/nerve conduction study","Bone scan with SPECT imaging only"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Tibial stress fracture: point tenderness over bone (especially the posterior medial tibia), pain with activity relieved by rest. X-ray is often NORMAL for 2-4 weeks — MRI is the most sensitive imaging for early stress fractures. Treatment: relative rest, offloading, correction of training errors. **Board tip:** High-risk stress fractures (femoral neck, anterior tibial cortex, navicular, metatarsal V) require MRI and orthopedic referral due to risk of complete fracture. Female Athlete Triad (disordered eating, amenorrhea, low bone density) is a major risk factor."},

  # ===== NEONATAL (15) =====
  {"topic":"Neonatal","course":"D119","q":"A 36-hour-old breastfed newborn has a total serum bilirubin of 14 mg/dL. The infant is vigorous, feeding well, with normal exam. Using the Bhutani nomogram, this falls in the intermediate-high risk zone. What is the management?","a":["Immediate exchange transfusion","Intensive phototherapy — exposure to high-irradiance light, continue breastfeeding, recheck bilirubin in 4-6 hours","No treatment — this is physiologic jaundice, observe only","Stop breastfeeding and start formula"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Newborn jaundice management uses the Bhutani hour-specific bilirubin nomogram plotted against age in hours. Intermediate-high risk: phototherapy is indicated. Continue breastfeeding (dehydration worsens bilirubin). High-irradiance phototherapy is effective and safe. **Board tip:** Indications for exchange transfusion: bilirubin rising despite maximal phototherapy, bilirubin approaching threshold for kernicterus (based on risk factors), or acute bilirubin encephalopathy signs (hypotonia, poor suck, high-pitched cry, arching)."},
  {"topic":"Neonatal","course":"D119","q":"A 3-day-old term male undergoes circumcision. What are the key post-operative care instructions for parents?","a":["Apply hydrogen peroxide to the circumcision site 3x daily","Apply petroleum jelly (Vaseline) gauze with each diaper change for 3-5 days to prevent adhesions and sticking; expect yellow crust as normal healing","Leave completely uncovered — no dressing needed","Apply antibiotic ointment and change dressing every 2 hours"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Post-circumcision care: Apply petroleum jelly (Vaseline) or Vaseline gauze to the head of the penis with each diaper change for 3-5 days to prevent adhesion to the diaper. Yellow crusting/exudate is NORMAL healing, not infection. Full healing takes 7-10 days. **Board tip:** Concerning signs: significant bleeding that doesn't stop, inability to urinate within 6-8 hours after, purulent drainage, increasing swelling/redness. Fever within 24 hours is abnormal and warrants evaluation."},
  {"topic":"Neonatal","course":"D119","q":"A newborn is discovered on state metabolic screening (heel-stick) to have elevated phenylalanine levels. If untreated, what is the primary complication of phenylketonuria (PKU)?","a":["Metabolic acidosis","Severe intellectual disability and neurologic damage from phenylalanine accumulation","Cataracts and lens dislocation","Hemolytic anemia"],"correct":1,"rationale":"PKU (autosomal recessive, PAH enzyme deficiency): Phenylalanine accumulates, causing brain toxicity, severe intellectual disability, behavior problems, and seizures if untreated. Treatment: phenylalanine-restricted diet started within 2 weeks of birth prevents neurologic damage — must be maintained lifelong. **Board tip:** The PKU heel-stick test is why newborn screening exists. Babies appear normal at birth — symptoms develop over months. A 'mouse-like' odor (phenylacetic acid) and fair complexion (tyrosine deficiency) are classic features of untreated PKU."},
  {"topic":"Neonatal","course":"D119","q":"A 28-day-old infant presents with 5 days of projectile, non-bilious vomiting after every feed. He is hungry after vomiting. Physical exam reveals a palpable olive-shaped mass in the right upper quadrant. What is the diagnosis?","a":["Gastroesophageal reflux","Pyloric stenosis — confirm with ultrasound, treat with pyloromyotomy (Ramstedt procedure)","Duodenal atresia — 'double bubble' sign","Intussusception"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis: Classic presentation in male infants 2-8 weeks old. Non-bilious projectile vomiting, hungry after vomiting, hypochloremic hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis (from repeated HCl loss), 'olive' mass, visible peristaltic waves. Ultrasound is diagnostic (pyloric muscle thickness >3mm, length >15mm). Treatment: IV rehydration first, then surgical pyloromyotomy. **Board tip:** Non-bilious vomiting = obstruction above the ampulla of Vater. Bilious vomiting = obstruction below the ampulla — more ominous (malrotation, duodenal atresia)."},
  {"topic":"Neonatal","course":"D119","q":"A term newborn's APGAR score is assessed at 1 minute: heart rate 90, weak cry, flexion posture, grimace only, pink body with blue extremities. What is the APGAR score?","a":["4","6","8","10"],"correct":1,"rationale":"APGAR scoring: Appearance (2=pink all over, 1=acrocyanosis, 0=blue/pale) = 1; Pulse (2=≥100, 1=<100, 0=absent) = 1; Grimace (2=cry, 1=grimace, 0=no response) = 1; Activity (2=active, 1=flexion, 0=limp) = 1; Respiration (2=strong cry, 1=weak, 0=absent) = 1. Total = 6. **Board tip:** APGAR at 1 min assesses need for resuscitation; APGAR at 5 min indicates effectiveness. Score 7-10 = normal; 4-6 = moderate depression; 0-3 = severe depression. Reassessed at 10 min if score <7 at 5 min."},
  {"topic":"Neonatal","course":"D119","q":"A newborn is found to have undescended testes bilaterally (cryptorchidism). At what age should surgical correction (orchiopexy) be performed if spontaneous descent does not occur?","a":["Immediately at birth","Between 6-18 months of age","After age 5 — many descend later","Wait until puberty"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Orchiopexy timing: Spontaneous descent occurs in 70% of cases by 3 months (up to 6 months). If not descended by 6 months, orchiopexy should be performed between 6-18 months. Early orchiopexy reduces risks of: infertility, testicular torsion, and possibly testicular cancer. **Board tip:** Undescended testes increase testicular cancer risk 3-5x — orchiopexy does NOT eliminate cancer risk but may reduce it and allows for easier self-exam surveillance. Bilateral cryptorchidism requires testosterone evaluation and chromosome karyotype (may indicate DSD/disorder of sex development)."},
  {"topic":"Neonatal","course":"D119","q":"A newborn girl has a vaginal discharge with slight blood-tinged secretions at day 3 of life. The mother is alarmed. What is the explanation?","a":["This indicates a vaginal infection requiring antibiotics","Physiologic vaginal discharge from maternal estrogen withdrawal — completely normal, resolves within 1-2 weeks","Trauma during delivery requiring pelvic exam","Clotting disorder — check CBC and coagulation studies"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Physiologic vaginal discharge (and rarely slight bleeding) in newborn girls is caused by abrupt withdrawal of maternal estrogen after birth. It is completely benign and self-resolving within 1-2 weeks. Reassurance is the only intervention needed. **Board tip:** Other normal newborn findings that alarm parents: breast enlargement (gynecomastia, both sexes), milia (tiny white facial bumps), erythema toxicum (blotchy red rash with yellow/white papules — most common newborn rash), mongolian spots (blue-gray sacral discoloration), physiologic jaundice."},
  {"topic":"Neonatal","course":"D119","q":"A 5-day-old breastfed newborn has not had a stool in 2 days. Birth weight was 3.5 kg, current weight 3.3 kg (94% of birth weight). He is latching well and has 6-8 wet diapers/day. What is the management?","a":["Immediate workup for Hirschsprung disease","Reassurance — breastfed infants can go several days without stooling after meconium passes","Glycerin suppository every day","Switch to formula immediately"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Breastfed newborns typically have frequent meconium stools initially, then may have variable stooling patterns. After the first week, breastfed infants may go 1-7 days between stools — this is normal as breast milk is highly digestible with little residue. Adequate wet diapers (6-8/day) confirm adequate intake. **Board tip:** Hirschsprung disease: failure to pass meconium within 48 hours of birth, abdominal distension, bilious vomiting, ribbon stools — warrants workup (rectal biopsy is gold standard). Normal healthy breastfed infants can stool once a week."},
  {"topic":"Neonatal","course":"D119","q":"Newborn hearing screening is recommended universally. Which is the currently recommended protocol?","a":["Auditory Brainstem Response (ABR) testing at 6 months","Universal newborn hearing screening before hospital discharge (1-3-6 rule: screen by 1 month, diagnose by 3 months, intervention by 6 months)","Screening only for at-risk infants (family history, NICU admission)","Behavioral hearing test at 12 months"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Universal newborn hearing screening (UNHS): 1-3-6 rule — screen by 1 month, complete diagnostic audiological evaluation by 3 months, early intervention by 6 months. Screening methods: OAE (otoacoustic emissions) or ABR (automated). Congenital hearing loss occurs in 1-3/1,000 newborns — early identification and intervention are critical for language development. **Board tip:** NICU infants should have ABR (not OAE) screening because OAE misses auditory neuropathy spectrum disorder (ANSD), which is more common in NICU graduates."},
  {"topic":"Neonatal","course":"D119","q":"A newborn has a white reflex (leukocoria) on the red reflex exam during the newborn exam. What is the most urgent concern?","a":["Normal variant — white sclera is common","Retinoblastoma (or congenital cataract) — urgent ophthalmology referral within days","Conjunctivitis — antibiotic drops","Subconjunctival hemorrhage from delivery — reassurance"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Leukocoria (white pupillary reflex, absent red reflex): This is an URGENT finding — must rule out retinoblastoma (most common intraocular tumor in children). Also caused by congenital cataract (another urgent cause, as untreated leads to amblyopia). **Board tip:** Red reflex exam should be performed at EVERY well-child visit. Absent or asymmetric red reflex requires urgent (same day or next day) ophthalmology referral. Retinoblastoma is treatable if caught early (5-year survival >95% with early treatment)."},
  {"topic":"Neonatal","course":"D119","q":"Erythromycin ophthalmic ointment is administered to all newborns at birth. What infection does this prevent?","a":["Herpes simplex virus (HSV) conjunctivitis","Ophthalmia neonatorum from Neisseria gonorrhoeae and Chlamydia trachomatis","Staphylococcal conjunctivitis","Cytomegalovirus (CMV) retinitis"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Erythromycin 0.5% ophthalmic ointment is mandated in most states to prevent ophthalmia neonatorum (gonococcal conjunctivitis) from Neisseria gonorrhoeae, which can cause corneal perforation and blindness. It also has activity against Chlamydia trachomatis. **Board tip:** Gonococcal ophthalmia neonatorum appears within 24-48 hours of birth. Chlamydial conjunctivitis appears at 5-14 days. Both acquired during vaginal delivery from infected mother. GC conjunctivitis requires IV/IM ceftriaxone — topical alone is insufficient for GC."},
  {"topic":"Neonatal","course":"D119","q":"A newborn of a mother with poorly controlled gestational diabetes is born at 38 weeks. At 2 hours of life, the infant is jittery with a blood glucose of 38 mg/dL. What is the management?","a":["Insulin drip immediately","Oral feeding (breast or formula) and recheck glucose in 1 hour; IV dextrose if glucose <40 despite feeding","IV dextrose bolus only — no oral feeding","Observation only — this is physiologic and will self-correct"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Neonatal hypoglycemia (blood glucose <40 mg/dL in symptomatic neonate): IDM (infant of diabetic mother) is at high risk due to fetal hyperinsulinism. Management: oral feeding first if infant can feed; recheck glucose 30 min after feed. IV dextrose (D10W 2 mL/kg) for symptomatic infants or glucose <40 despite feeding. **Board tip:** IDM risks: hypoglycemia, hypocalcemia, polycythemia, macrosomia, cardiomegaly, respiratory distress syndrome, hyperbilirubinemia. Screen glucose at 30 min, 1 hour, 2 hours of life in at-risk infants."},
  {"topic":"Neonatal","course":"D119","q":"Vitamin K prophylaxis is given to all newborns at birth. What condition does it prevent?","a":["Rickets and bone weakness","Vitamin K deficiency bleeding (VKDB) — formerly hemorrhagic disease of the newborn — including potentially fatal intracranial hemorrhage","Neonatal sepsis","Retinopathy of prematurity"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Vitamin K deficiency bleeding (VKDB): Newborns have very low vitamin K stores (poor placental transfer) and breast milk has minimal vitamin K. Without prophylaxis, VKDB causes GI bleeding, intracranial hemorrhage, and death. IM vitamin K 1 mg at birth is highly effective prevention. **Board tip:** Parents who refuse vitamin K (common in some communities): counsel that breast milk is low in vitamin K, and intracranial hemorrhage from VKDB can occur in 1/10,000 unprotected infants. The IM route is preferred over oral (more reliable absorption). This is a child safety/advocacy issue."},
  {"topic":"Neonatal","course":"D119","q":"A 2-day-old infant's total serum bilirubin is 18 mg/dL. Mother is blood type O+, infant is type A+. Direct Coombs test is positive. What is the most likely cause of this jaundice?","a":["Physiologic jaundice","ABO incompatibility — maternal anti-A and anti-B IgG antibodies crossing the placenta and causing hemolysis","Rh incompatibility — mother is Rh positive so cannot occur","Breast milk jaundice"],"correct":1,"rationale":"ABO incompatibility: Most common hemolytic disease of the newborn. Occurs when mother is type O (has anti-A and anti-B IgG antibodies that cross the placenta) and infant is type A or B. Positive direct Coombs (DAT) confirms maternal antibody-coated fetal RBCs. **Board tip:** ABO incompatibility can occur in the FIRST pregnancy (unlike Rh disease). Rh incompatibility: mother Rh negative, infant Rh positive — prevented by RhoGAM. ABO: mother type O, infant type A or B — more common, usually milder than Rh disease."},
  {"topic":"Neonatal","course":"D119","q":"A newborn has a sacral dimple noted on physical exam. What feature of the dimple REQUIRES spinal imaging (spinal ultrasound or MRI)?","a":["Any sacral dimple in any infant","Dimple located >2.5 cm from the anus, size >5mm, associated with a tuft of hair, skin tag, cutaneous hemangioma, or skin discoloration (red flags for spinal dysraphism)","Dimple in any male infant","Dimple at the very tip of the coccyx"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Simple sacral dimple (≤5mm, ≤2.5 cm from anus, no associated features) = normal variant, no imaging needed. RED FLAGS requiring spinal ultrasound (age <3 months) or MRI: dimple >2.5 cm from anus (higher location), size >5mm, associated hair tuft, skin tag, hemangioma, lipoma, or cutaneous discoloration — these suggest occult spinal dysraphism (tethered cord, spina bifida occulta). **Board tip:** Spinal ultrasound in infants <3 months (before spinal ossification obscures the cord) is the preferred first imaging. MRI is used if ultrasound is equivocal or infant is older."},

  # ===== MENTAL HEALTH PEDIATRIC (15) =====
  {"topic":"Mental Health Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"The USPSTF recommends screening for major depressive disorder (MDD) in adolescents starting at age 12. Which validated tool is commonly used in primary care?","a":["CAGE questionnaire","Patient Health Questionnaire for Adolescents (PHQ-A) or PHQ-9","Beck Depression Inventory (not appropriate for primary care screening)","MMSE"],"correct":1,"rationale":"PHQ-A (Patient Health Questionnaire — Adolescent version) or PHQ-9 is the recommended validated screening tool for adolescent depression in primary care settings. USPSTF recommends screening adolescents 12-18 years when adequate systems are in place for follow-up. **Board tip:** PHQ-9 score interpretation: 0-4=minimal, 5-9=mild, 10-14=moderate, 15-19=moderately severe, ≥20=severe depression. Question 9 (suicidal ideation) must always be addressed regardless of total score."},
  {"topic":"Mental Health Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 15-year-old is diagnosed with major depressive disorder (MDD). Medications are being considered. What does the FDA black box warning on SSRIs in patients under 25 years state?","a":["SSRIs are absolutely contraindicated in all children and adolescents","Increased risk of suicidal thinking and behavior (not completed suicide) in the first few weeks of treatment — requires close monitoring","SSRIs cause liver failure in adolescents","SSRIs are associated with permanent cognitive impairment"],"correct":1,"rationale":"FDA black box warning: SSRIs (and SNRIs) increase risk of SUICIDAL THINKING AND BEHAVIOR (not completed suicide) in children, adolescents, and young adults (under 25) during the first weeks of treatment. This should NOT prevent appropriate treatment — untreated depression has far greater suicide risk. **Board tip:** Fluoxetine (Prozac) is FDA-approved for MDD in children ≥8 years and is first-line. Escitalopram approved ≥12 years. Safety monitoring: weekly follow-up for first 4 weeks, then biweekly x 4 weeks, then monthly."},
  {"topic":"Mental Health Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 14-year-old girl has been restricting food intake, has lost 15% of her body weight, has amenorrhea, and distorted body image (perceives herself as fat). What is the most medically dangerous immediate complication requiring hospitalization?","a":["Osteoporosis","Bradycardia (HR <50) or hypotension — cardiovascular complications are the most dangerous","Lanugo hair","Hyperkalemia"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Anorexia nervosa: The most medically dangerous psychiatric disorder (highest mortality of any mental illness). Hospitalization criteria include HR <50 bpm, BP <90/60, orthostatic hypotension, electrolyte imbalances (especially hypokalemia, hypophosphatemia from refeeding syndrome), severe weight loss. **Board tip:** Medical complications of anorexia: Bradycardia (most dangerous — arrhythmia risk), hypotension, QTc prolongation, osteoporosis, lanugo, hypothermia, leukopenia. Refeeding syndrome (hypophosphatemia, cardiac failure) occurs when nutrition is reintroduced too rapidly."},
  {"topic":"Mental Health Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 16-year-old presents with recurrent binge eating followed by self-induced vomiting. She has eroded dental enamel, calluses on her knuckles (Russell's sign), and hypertrophy of the parotid glands. What is the diagnosis?","a":["Anorexia nervosa, binge-purge subtype","Bulimia nervosa — fluoxetine and cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) are first-line treatment","Binge eating disorder","Pica"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Bulimia nervosa: recurrent binge-purge cycles, weight often normal or near-normal. Classic physical signs: dental erosions (from gastric acid), Russell's sign (calluses on dorsum of hand from self-induced vomiting), parotid gland enlargement. Treatment: CBT (most effective) + fluoxetine (FDA-approved for bulimia, 60 mg/day). **Board tip:** Fluoxetine 60 mg (higher than depression dose) is the only FDA-approved medication for bulimia. Metabolic complications: hypokalemia, hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis (from vomiting), or acidosis (from laxative abuse)."},
  {"topic":"Mental Health Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"During a well-child visit, a 13-year-old expresses that she feels sad most of the time, has lost interest in friends, sleeps 12 hours daily, and has had thoughts of 'not wanting to be here.' What is the MOST important immediate action?","a":["Schedule follow-up in 2 weeks","Complete a suicide risk assessment immediately (intent, plan, means, access) and determine level of care needed","Start fluoxetine today without further evaluation","Refer to psychology and return in 1 month"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Any expression of passive suicidal ideation ('not wanting to be here') requires immediate, structured suicide risk assessment: Does she have a plan? Intent? Access to means (especially firearms)? Previous attempts? Protective factors? Based on this assessment, determine: can she be safely managed as outpatient, or does she need urgent psychiatric care/ER? **Board tip:** Means restriction counseling is critical — ask about firearms in the home. Secure lethal means (lock medications, secure firearms). Lethal means counseling reduces suicide risk. Document risk assessment thoroughly."},
  {"topic":"Mental Health Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"Which evidence-based psychotherapy is considered first-line for pediatric anxiety disorders (generalized anxiety, social anxiety, separation anxiety)?","a":["Psychodynamic therapy","Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) — with or without SSRI depending on severity","EMDR (Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing)","Art therapy alone"],"correct":1,"rationale":"CBT is the gold-standard, evidence-based treatment for pediatric anxiety disorders, utilizing exposure-based techniques, cognitive restructuring, and relaxation skills. For mild-moderate anxiety: CBT alone. For moderate-severe: CBT + SSRI (fluoxetine, sertraline are most evidence-based in children). **Board tip:** Combined CBT + SSRI is most effective for moderate-severe pediatric anxiety (CAMS study). SSRIs alone are less effective without therapy. Buspirone is sometimes used for GAD but has less evidence in pediatrics."},
  {"topic":"Mental Health Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 17-year-old male has had episodes of dramatically elevated mood, decreased sleep need, racing thoughts, increased goal-directed activity, and grandiosity lasting 5-7 days without causing severe impairment or requiring hospitalization. He then has a 3-week depressive episode. What is the diagnosis?","a":["Bipolar I disorder","Bipolar II disorder (hypomania + major depressive episodes)","Cyclothymia","Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder (DMDD)"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Bipolar II: Requires at least one hypomanic episode (elevated mood ≥4 days, not severe enough to require hospitalization — distinguishes from mania) + at least one MDE. Bipolar I: Full manic episode (≥7 days OR requires hospitalization OR psychotic features). **Board tip:** DMDD (Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder) is a DSM-5 diagnosis for children with chronic irritability and severe recurrent temper outbursts — NOT episodic mood changes. This diagnosis was created to prevent over-diagnosis of bipolar disorder in children."},
  {"topic":"Mental Health Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"What percentage of U.S. teenagers report experiencing a major depressive episode, and which population has disproportionately higher rates?","a":["2% — all groups are equal","~13-17% of adolescents; adolescent girls have rates nearly 3x higher than boys; LGBTQ+ youth have substantially higher rates","<1% — adolescent depression is rare","5% — equally distributed across all demographics"],"correct":1,"rationale":"MDD affects approximately 13-17% of adolescents, with girls having rates nearly 3x higher than boys after puberty. LGBTQ+ youth have significantly elevated rates of depression, anxiety, and suicide attempts compared to their heterosexual peers (due to minority stress, discrimination, family rejection). **Board tip:** LGBTQ+ youth are a high-risk population — screen more frequently and ensure a supportive, affirming clinical environment. Family acceptance is the most powerful protective factor for LGBTQ+ youth mental health."},
  {"topic":"Mental Health Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A mother asks about her 8-year-old son who has been having panic attacks — sudden heart racing, sweating, shortness of breath, and fear of dying. He now refuses to go to school. Which approach is most appropriate?","a":["Dismiss concerns — children cannot have panic disorder","Diagnose school refusal only — panic attacks are a secondary concern","Diagnose panic disorder with potential agoraphobia; initiate CBT with exposure therapy; consider SSRI for moderate-severe cases","Prescribe benzodiazepine for acute panic attacks"],"correct":2,"rationale":"Panic disorder CAN occur in children. Hallmarks: recurrent unexpected panic attacks + persistent concern about future attacks AND/OR avoidance (school refusal). CBT (especially interoceptive exposure) is first-line. SSRIs for moderate-severe. **Board tip:** Benzodiazepines are generally AVOIDED in pediatric anxiety/panic — they create dependence and do not teach coping skills. CBT equips children with lifelong skills. Lorazepam may be used very short-term for acute crisis only."},
  {"topic":"Mental Health Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 15-year-old is suspected of having an eating disorder based on dramatic weight loss and restricted eating. Which initial laboratory workup is most important?","a":["Thyroid panel only","CBC, CMP (electrolytes, BUN, creatinine, glucose), LFTs, phosphorus, magnesium, EKG, and thyroid function tests","Fasting lipid panel","Urine hCG only"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Initial labs for suspected eating disorder: CBC (anemia, leukopenia), CMP (electrolytes — especially potassium, chloride for purging; BUN/Cr for dehydration; glucose), LFTs, phosphorus and magnesium (refeeding syndrome risk), EKG (QTc prolongation, bradycardia, arrhythmia), TSH (rule out hyperthyroidism causing weight loss). **Board tip:** The EKG is critical — identify dangerous arrhythmias before beginning refeeding. Bradycardia is the most common cardiac finding. QTc >450 ms warrants cardiac monitoring."},
  {"topic":"Mental Health Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 14-year-old is brought in after being found cutting her forearms with a razor. She denies wanting to die, stating it 'relieves emotional pain.' What type of behavior is this and what is the appropriate clinical response?","a":["This is a suicide attempt — immediate psychiatric hospitalization","Non-suicidal self-injury (NSSI) — assess for suicidal ideation (NSSI ≠ suicide attempt), evaluate for underlying psychiatric disorder, initiate therapy, safety planning","Ignore — it is 'attention-seeking' behavior","Diagnose as borderline personality disorder and refer only"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Non-suicidal self-injury (NSSI): self-harm without suicidal intent, used to regulate overwhelming emotions. It is NOT the same as a suicide attempt but is a significant risk factor for future suicide. Management: assess for suicidal ideation separately, evaluate for depression/BPH/trauma, initiate DBT (Dialectical Behavior Therapy) which is the most evidence-based treatment for NSSI. **Board tip:** NSSI significantly increases lifetime suicide risk. Ask directly about suicidal intent — never assume NSSI = no suicide risk. DBT teaches distress tolerance and emotional regulation skills."},
  {"topic":"Mental Health Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 16-year-old is brought to the clinic after being found unconscious at a party after drinking alcohol. He is embarrassed and dismissive. Which validated screening tool is appropriate for adolescent alcohol and substance use in primary care?","a":["CAGE questionnaire (designed for adults)","CRAFFT (Car, Relax, Alone, Forget, Friends, Trouble) screening tool — validated for adolescents 12-21 years","AUDIT-C only","DAST-10"],"correct":1,"rationale":"CRAFFT (Car, Relax, Alone, Forget, Friends, Trouble) is the validated screening tool for adolescent substance use in primary care, specifically designed for ages 12-21. Score ≥2 = high risk, warrants brief intervention and referral if needed. CAGE and AUDIT are adult tools. **Board tip:** CRAFFT question mnemonic: Have you ever ridden in a CAR driven by someone (including yourself) who was using alcohol/drugs? Do you use to RELAX? Alone? FORGET things? Do FRIENDS/family say you should cut down? Have you gotten into TROUBLE?"},
  {"topic":"Mental Health Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 7-year-old exhibits repetitive, purposeless motor behaviors (eye blinking, head jerking, throat clearing) that come and go, last <12 months. He is otherwise healthy. What is the most appropriate management?","a":["Immediate neurology referral for seizure evaluation","Provisional tic disorder — reassurance, psychoeducation; watchful waiting as most resolve; treat only if tics cause significant distress or functional impairment","Start haloperidol immediately","MRI brain stat"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Provisional tic disorder: simple transient motor and/or vocal tics present <12 months. Very common in school-age children (10-20%). Most resolve spontaneously. Management: psychoeducation, reassurance, watchful waiting. Treat if tics are severe, frequent, painful, or causing significant social/academic impairment. **Board tip:** Tourette syndrome = multiple motor tics + ≥1 vocal tic for >1 year, not necessarily concurrent. Treatment options for persistent tics: behavior therapy (CBIT), guanfacine, clonidine, fluphenazine, or aripiprazole."},
  {"topic":"Mental Health Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 12-year-old reports being bullied daily at school, resulting in school avoidance. He has symptoms of depression and social anxiety. Which USPSTF-recommended intervention is essential in addition to mental health treatment?","a":["Immediate school transfer","Involve school counselors, document bullying as adverse childhood experience (ACE), address depression and anxiety with CBT and possibly SSRI, and provide family psychoeducation","Prescribe benzodiazepines for school days","Advise homeschooling"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Bullying is an adverse childhood experience (ACE) with documented mental health consequences (depression, anxiety, PTSD, suicidality). ACE documentation is important for clinical context. Management requires coordinated approach: school communication, mental health treatment (CBT for depression and anxiety), family education, and safety planning. **Board tip:** ACEs (Adverse Childhood Experiences) — include abuse, neglect, household dysfunction, bullying. Dose-response relationship: more ACEs = higher risk of mental and physical health problems in adulthood. USPSTF recommends clinical awareness and documentation."},
  {"topic":"Mental Health Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"During a 15-year-old male's well visit, the FNP should address confidentiality. Which topics can a minor discuss confidentially with a provider in most states WITHOUT parental notification?","a":["All medical information — parents have no rights","Reproductive health (contraception, STI testing/treatment), mental health counseling, and substance use treatment","Parents always have complete access to all information","Only immunization records are confidential"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Minor consent laws vary by state, but most states allow minors to consent to and receive confidential care for: STI testing/treatment, contraception, mental health counseling, and substance abuse treatment. Exceptions: mandatory reporting (abuse, imminent safety threats) requires breach of confidentiality. **Board tip:** HEEADSSS assessment for adolescents: Home, Education/Employment, Eating, Activities, Drugs, Sex, Suicide/Depression, Safety. Should be conducted privately (without parents). Establishing confidentiality increases adolescent disclosure and preventive care engagement."},

  # ===== DERMATOLOGY PEDIATRIC (15) =====
  {"topic":"Dermatology Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 6-month-old presents with dry, scaly, intensely pruritic patches on the cheeks, scalp, and extensor surfaces. Family history is positive for allergic rhinitis and asthma. What is the diagnosis and first-line treatment?","a":["Psoriasis — refer to dermatology","Atopic dermatitis (eczema) — gentle moisturizers, mild topical steroids (hydrocortisone 1% for face), avoid triggers, daily bathing with emollient application within 3 minutes (soak and seal)","Contact dermatitis — remove the allergen only","Seborrheic dermatitis — antifungal shampoo"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Atopic dermatitis: chronic inflammatory skin condition, associated with atopic triad (asthma, allergic rhinitis, eczema). Infantile eczema: cheeks, scalp, extensor surfaces. Older children: flexural surfaces. Treatment: emollients (cornerstone), topical corticosteroids (low-potency for face/intertriginous areas), avoid triggers (dry air, wool, harsh soaps, sweating). **Board tip:** Soak and seal method: daily lukewarm bath → immediately apply topical steroid to active lesions → seal with thick emollient (Vaseline, CeraVe) within 3 minutes. Eczema = gateway to asthma and allergic rhinitis (atopic march)."},
  {"topic":"Dermatology Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 10-year-old with atopic dermatitis has facial involvement not responding to hydrocortisone 1%. What is the next step?","a":["Systemic oral prednisone","Topical calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus 0.03% or pimecrolimus 1%) for steroid-sparing treatment on face and intertriginous areas","Systemic cyclosporine","Phototherapy"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Topical calcineurin inhibitors (TCIs — tacrolimus, pimecrolimus): Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory agents approved for atopic dermatitis as second-line therapy, especially useful for the FACE and intertriginous areas where potent steroids cause skin atrophy. **Board tip:** Tacrolimus 0.03% and pimecrolimus are FDA-approved for children ≥2 years. They carry an FDA black box warning about theoretical cancer risk (based on animal data) — use minimum amount needed and avoid in immunocompromised patients. For moderate-severe AD not controlled with topicals, consider dupilumab (Dupixent, FDA-approved ≥6 months)."},
  {"topic":"Dermatology Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 4-year-old has golden-crusted, honey-colored lesions around his mouth and nose. Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in clusters. What is the causative organism and treatment?","a":["Streptococcus pyogenes — penicillin","Staphylococcus aureus (most common) — topical mupirocin for localized; oral cephalexin or dicloxacillin for extensive/bullous impetigo","Candida albicans — topical antifungal","Herpes simplex — acyclovir"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Non-bullous impetigo: predominantly Staph aureus (some GAS). Honey-colored crusted lesions around nose/mouth. Highly contagious (direct contact). Treatment: topical mupirocin (limited disease) → oral cephalexin (extensive or bullous impetigo, or suspected MRSA: use TMP-SMX). **Board tip:** Bullous impetigo (fluid-filled bullae) is caused exclusively by Staph aureus toxin-producing strains (phage group II, toxin cleaves desmoglein-1). Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome (SSSS) is a systemic form with widespread blistering — requires IV antibiotics."},
  {"topic":"Dermatology Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"An 8-year-old boy has a scaly, circular patch on his scalp with broken-off hair shafts (black dots), mild erythema, and cervical lymphadenopathy. KOH preparation shows fungal hyphae and spores. What is the treatment?","a":["Topical clotrimazole cream","Oral griseofulvin (first-line for tinea capitis) — topical antifungals are INEFFECTIVE for scalp dermatophytosis","Oral fluconazole for 1 day","Topical selenium sulfide shampoo alone"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Tinea capitis (ringworm of the scalp): Trichophyton tonsurans (most common in U.S., endothrix — spores inside hair shaft → black dot sign) or Microsporum canis. ORAL antifungals are required — topical doesn't penetrate hair follicles. First-line: griseofulvin (microsize 20-25 mg/kg/day x 6-8 weeks). Alternative: terbinafine, fluconazole, itraconazole. **Board tip:** Adjunctive selenium sulfide or ketoconazole shampoo 2x/week reduces spore shedding and transmission. Treat household contacts. Cervical lymphadenopathy = kerion (inflammatory tinea capitis) or just reactive nodes — large kerion may need a short course of oral steroids."},
  {"topic":"Dermatology Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 7-year-old has multiple (12-15) small, flesh-colored, dome-shaped, umbilicated papules on the trunk and arms. They are non-tender and have been present for 3 months. What is the diagnosis and management?","a":["Warts (verruca vulgaris) — cryotherapy immediately","Molluscum contagiosum (Poxvirus) — watchful waiting (most resolve in 6-12 months) is first-line in immunocompetent children","Chickenpox — acyclovir","Folliculitis — antibiotics"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Molluscum contagiosum: Poxvirus, common in children 1-12 years. Classic: small, firm, umbilicated (central dimple) skin-colored papules, 2-5mm diameter, commonly on trunk/extremities. Spread by skin-to-skin contact or autoinoculation. Immunocompetent children: self-limited (resolves in 6-18 months). Treatment is elective for cosmetic/social concerns: cantharidin (applied in office), cryotherapy, curettage. **Board tip:** Molluscum in atopic dermatitis can be extensive ('molluscum dermatitis'). Genital molluscum in children may indicate sexual abuse — evaluate carefully. Disseminated/large molluscum = consider immunodeficiency."},
  {"topic":"Dermatology Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"Parents bring a 5-year-old with intensely pruritic burrows between the fingers, on the wrists, and in the genital area. The child's daycare classmate was recently diagnosed with the same condition. What is the treatment?","a":["Oral antihistamines and moisturizers only","Permethrin 5% cream applied to entire body from neck down, left on for 8-14 hours, then washed off — ALL household members treated simultaneously","Topical hydrocortisone","Oral griseofulvin"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Scabies (Sarcoptes scabiei): intense pruritus worse at night, burrows (pathognomonic) in interdigital web spaces, wrists, genitalia. Treatment: permethrin 5% cream, applied neck-down, left 8-14 hours, repeat in 1 week. ALL household members and close contacts treated simultaneously, regardless of symptoms (incubation 4-6 weeks before itch). **Board tip:** Launder all clothing and bedding in hot water. In infants <2 months: use 6% sulfur in petroleum (permethrin not approved <2 months). Norwegian/crusted scabies: hyperkeratotic plaques, extremely contagious, requires oral ivermectin + topical permethrin."},
  {"topic":"Dermatology Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 16-year-old presents with open comedones (blackheads) and closed comedones (whiteheads) on her forehead and nose, with a few small papules. She has no nodules or cysts. What is the first-line treatment for comedonal/mild papular acne?","a":["Oral isotretinoin (Accutane)","Topical retinoid (tretinoin 0.025% or adapalene 0.1% gel) ± benzoyl peroxide","Oral tetracycline","Oral spironolactone"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Mild acne (comedonal and mild papular): first-line = topical retinoid (adapalene or tretinoin) for comedolytic effect PLUS topical benzoyl peroxide (bactericidal, prevents resistance). Topical antibiotics (clindamycin) added for papular/pustular acne ONLY in combination with BP (never alone — causes resistance). **Board tip:** Acne treatment ladder: Mild (topical retinoid + BP) → Moderate papular/pustular (add topical/oral antibiotic) → Severe nodular/cystic (isotretinoin referral, or spironolactone for females). Isotretinoin requires iPLEDGE program (pregnancy prevention) due to severe teratogenicity."},
  {"topic":"Dermatology Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 9-year-old with well-controlled atopic dermatitis develops a sudden flare with widespread vesicles and punched-out erosions overlying his eczematous patches. He has fever and is irritable. What is the most likely diagnosis?","a":["Bacterial superinfection with MRSA","Eczema herpeticum (Kaposi varicelliform eruption) — Herpes Simplex Virus superinfection of atopic dermatitis","Contact dermatitis","Drug reaction"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Eczema herpeticum: HSV infection superimposed on atopic dermatitis. Classic: sudden widespread vesicles with punched-out erosions (umbilicated vesicles that rupture) on pre-existing eczematous skin, fever, lymphadenopathy. Can be life-threatening — requires URGENT treatment with oral acyclovir (outpatient mild) or IV acyclovir (severe/disseminated). **Board tip:** Eczema herpeticum is a medical emergency. Do NOT use topical corticosteroids during HSV flare. This is a board-critical complication of atopic dermatitis. Bacterial superinfection (Staph, Strep) also common in eczema — crusting, oozing, honey-colored crust."},
  {"topic":"Dermatology Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 3-year-old has a moist, beefy-red diaper rash with satellite lesions extending beyond the main rash edges. The rash has not responded to regular diaper creams. What is the diagnosis and treatment?","a":["Contact/irritant dermatitis — increase zinc oxide cream use","Candidal diaper dermatitis — topical antifungal (nystatin or clotrimazole) with each diaper change","Psoriasis — topical steroid","Bacterial cellulitis — oral antibiotics"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Candidal diaper rash: Beefy-red erythema with sharp borders and SATELLITE LESIONS (small pustules or papules beyond the main rash edge — pathognomonic) in skin folds. Does NOT respond to barrier creams alone. Treatment: topical antifungal (nystatin or clotrimazole) with every diaper change for 7-14 days. **Board tip:** Combination products (antifungal + potent steroid) are NOT recommended for diaper area — potent steroids cause skin atrophy in diaper area. Use antifungal alone. Satellite lesions are the KEY distinguishing feature from irritant diaper dermatitis."},
  {"topic":"Dermatology Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"An 11-year-old has a single 2 cm oval, slightly scaly, 'herald patch' on her trunk, followed 1-2 weeks later by multiple smaller oval scaly plaques arranged along skin tension lines in a 'Christmas tree' pattern. The rash is mildly pruritic. What is the diagnosis?","a":["Tinea corporis — topical antifungal","Pityriasis rosea — self-limited, no treatment needed; oral erythromycin or acyclovir for severe cases","Secondary syphilis — RPR","Drug reaction"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Pityriasis rosea: Possibly caused by HHV-6 or HHV-7 reactivation. Classic: single herald patch (leading edge with collarette scale) → Christmas tree pattern of smaller plaques following Langer lines on trunk. Self-limited, resolves in 6-12 weeks. Usually mildly pruritic. **Board tip:** SECONDARY SYPHILIS can mimic pityriasis rosea BUT includes palms/soles involvement — always check palms and soles! If sexually active adolescent with PR-like rash + palms/soles involvement → RPR/VDRL immediately. Pityriasis rosea spares palms/soles."},
  {"topic":"Dermatology Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 14-year-old presents with target-like lesions (concentric rings of different colors) on the palms, soles, and mucous membranes (mouth and eyes), with widespread blistering. She recently completed amoxicillin for strep throat. What is the diagnosis?","a":["Varicella — acyclovir","Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) — emergency: stop offending drug, dermatology/ophthalmology consult, wound care, IV fluids","Bullous impetigo — IV antibiotics","Erythema multiforme minor — self-limited"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS): Severe mucocutaneous reaction. Bullous detachment <10% BSA = SJS; >30% = Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN). Common triggers: antibiotics (amoxicillin, TMP-SMX), anticonvulsants, NSAIDs. Mucosal involvement + target lesions + blistering = SJS. EMERGENCY: stop offending drug, fluid resuscitation, wound care like burn patient, ophthalmology for eye involvement. **Board tip:** Erythema multiforme minor = small target lesions, mostly acral (hands/feet), minimal/no mucosal involvement, usually triggered by HSV — NOT SJS. SJS/TEN = severe systemic disease with mortality."},
  {"topic":"Dermatology Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 7-year-old has a 3 cm annular red plaque with central clearing on his forearm. The child's cat was recently treated for skin lesions. What is the diagnosis and treatment?","a":["Nummular eczema — topical steroids","Tinea corporis (ringworm) — topical terbinafine or clotrimazole x 2-4 weeks","Lyme disease — doxycycline","Granuloma annulare — no treatment"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Tinea corporis: Dermatophyte infection (Microsporum canis from cats/dogs, or Trichophyton). Classic: annular scaling plaque with central clearing (ringworm). Treatment: topical antifungal (terbinafine, clotrimazole, miconazole) x 2-4 weeks. **Board tip:** Distinguish from Lyme disease EM rash: Lyme EM does NOT have scale and is NOT itchy; it expands rapidly (>5 cm); associated with tick exposure. Tinea corporis is scaly, itchy, slowly expanding. Tinea requires KOH prep for definitive diagnosis if uncertain."},
  {"topic":"Dermatology Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 12-year-old develops a painful, indurated, red nodule on her central face (cheek). It is fluctuant on exam. What is the most appropriate treatment?","a":["Topical benzoyl peroxide alone","Incision and drainage (I&D) plus TMP-SMX to cover CA-MRSA — most skin abscesses in community settings are MRSA","Systemic prednisone","IV vancomycin"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Community-acquired skin abscess: CA-MRSA is now the predominant cause. Treatment: I&D is the primary treatment (drainage is curative for most uncomplicated abscesses). Adjunctive antibiotics (TMP-SMX or clindamycin based on local resistance) for: extensive cellulitis, systemic infection signs, facial abscesses, immunocompromised patients. **Board tip:** IDSA guidelines recommend I&D as the main intervention for uncomplicated skin abscesses. Antibiotics: TMP-SMX (Bactrim) DS BID x 5-7 days is preferred for CA-MRSA. MRSA is now the leading cause of purulent skin infections in children."},
  {"topic":"Dermatology Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 2-year-old has multiple hypopigmented, slightly scaly macules on her face and upper arms. They are more visible in summer when her skin tans. She is asymptomatic. What is the diagnosis?","a":["Vitiligo — autoimmune, refer to dermatology","Pityriasis alba — common, associated with atopic dermatitis; treat underlying eczema, moisturize, self-resolves","Tinea versicolor — selenium sulfide shampoo","Tuberous sclerosis — brain MRI"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Pityriasis alba: Common benign condition in children 3-16 years, associated with atopic dermatitis. Hypopigmented, slightly scaly oval patches most visible in dark-skinned individuals or after sun exposure. Benign, self-limited. Treatment: moisturizers for xerosis, treat underlying atopic dermatitis. Low-potency topical steroids or tacrolimus may help. **Board tip:** Distinguish from tinea versicolor (adolescents, trunk/shoulders, KOH shows 'spaghetti and meatballs' — hyphae and spores) and vitiligo (complete depigmentation with sharp borders, autoimmune). Pityriasis alba = partial hypopigmentation with indistinct borders."},
  {"topic":"Dermatology Pediatric","course":"D119","q":"A 15-year-old with severe nodulocystic acne (multiple large, painful cysts and nodules on face and back) has failed topical and oral antibiotics for 6 months. What is the definitive treatment?","a":["Increase oral antibiotic dose and continue for 12 more months","Oral isotretinoin (Accutane) — refer to dermatology; requires iPLEDGE program enrollment","Intralesional triamcinolone alone","Oral spironolactone (appropriate for females but not definitive for nodular)"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Isotretinoin (oral retinoid) is the only treatment that achieves long-term remission in severe nodulocystic/nodular acne (>90% clearance, many permanently). Mechanism: reduces sebum production, normalizes follicular keratinization, reduces P. acnes, anti-inflammatory. Requires iPLEDGE REMS due to severe teratogenicity (must use 2 forms of birth control in fertile females). **Board tip:** Isotretinoin side effects: teratogenicity (Category X), hepatotoxicity, hypertriglyceridemia, pseudotumor cerebri, myalgias, dry skin/lips. Lab monitoring: lipids and LFTs monthly. Do NOT combine with tetracyclines (increased ICP risk)."},
]

